STRIP(1)                  User Commands                   STRIP(1)

NAME
    strip - remove symbols and sections from ELF files

SYNOPSIS
    strip [options] file...

DESCRIPTION
    strip rewrites ELF64 files, and the ELF members of archives, without
    their symbols and debug sections. The input files are relocatable
    objects, executables and shared objects of x86_64 and aarch64 in
    little-endian format. Without -o the program replaces each file.

    Without an option for symbols the program acts as with -s. Executables
    and shared objects retain their program headers and the file offsets
    of all allocated sections, so the loaded image does not change. The
    section header table and the sections without the allocated flag
    follow the allocated contents.

    A relocatable object receives a new layout. The symbol table is
    numbered again, and every relocation section receives the new symbol
    indices. With -s the relocations against removed symbols disappear
    together with the symbols.

OPTIONS
    -s, --strip-all
        Remove all symbols and the debug sections. This is the default.
    -g, -S, -d, --strip-debug
        Remove the debug sections, the file symbols and the section
        symbols of relocatable objects that no relocation uses.
    --strip-unneeded
        Remove the debug sections and all symbols that no relocation or
        dynamic link needs.
    -R name, --remove-section=name
        Also remove the sections with this name. The option is repeatable.
    -N name, --strip-symbol=name
        Remove the symbols with this name unless a relocation uses them.
    -K name, --keep-symbol=name
        Do not remove the symbols with this name when another option
        removes them.
    -o file
        Write the result to file. The option accepts one input file.
    -p, --preserve-dates
        Give the output file the access and modification times of the
        input file.
    -I bfdname, -O bfdname
        Select the formats as in objcopy. The output must be an ELF file.

    The arguments of -R, -N and -K accept the wildcards * and ?.

EXAMPLES
    strip prog
    strip -g libx.a
    strip --strip-unneeded -o libx.small.so libx.so

SEE ALSO
    objcopy(1), readelf(1), ar(1), nm(1)
